 "id": "1917-thorndike-beauty",
 "name": "Beauty of simple forms ranking (Thorndike 1917 rectangles, triangles, crosses)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Edward L. Thorndike's order-of-merit experiment (Psychological Review 24(2), 1917, pp. 147–153). Participants rank three sets of twelve line figures from the one they \"like the looks of most\" to the one they like least: (A) rectangles 22–33 (same height, growing wider), (B) isosceles triangles 41–52 (same base, growing taller), (C) crosses 61–66 (bar one fourth from the top, growing longer) and 81–86 (bar about half the upright, placed lower). Figures are shown in the printed order with Thorndike's numbers, and each answer is the list of figure numbers in rank order, so results compare directly with his Table VI (rectangle 29 first, 22 and 33 last; triangles 43 and 44 first; crosses 64, 82, 65 first) and with his point that individual judgments vary enormously. Differences from the original study: (1) forms are redrawn on screen from dimensions measured on the scanned figures (a few percent measurement error), not cut or printed at double size; (2) the lettered designs (D) and the 24 unnumbered designs (E) are left out; (3) participants are not restricted to college juniors; gender, age and art training are asked at the end. Public domain source.",
